WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.750
Their similarity makes them
more difficult to learn.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.580
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann, and today
I have a slightly different subject.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.380
Uh, I spoke in a previous video about
how important it is to learn from things

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.300
that are familiar, but by the same token,
and the emphasis today is how difficult

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.970
it is to learn things that are similar.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.400
Now, if you enjoy my
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:24.405 --> 00:00:25.910
Click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.030
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:30.900
I do appreciate it.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.120
So what do I mean by that?

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.830
Well, I notice for example, that
whenever I start a new language, there

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.050
are words that sound very similar.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:47.850
Uh ... you know, one is change, the
other is understand in Japanese.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:52.500
It's very difficult to disentangle
these words that sound similar.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:01:00.300
Another example is uh, very often, um,
well meaning people who write textbooks or

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:07.890
teachers will try to get you to learn, you
know, in chapter three, the colors, uh,

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.550
I remember I had a teacher once telling
me a tutor online, say, today we're

00:01:11.554 --> 00:01:13.410
going to, we're gonna learn the colors.

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:18.360
It's very difficult because that, those
are similar, those are colors, and if

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:25.275
you try to learn the colors as a batch, I
personally find that very difficult to do.

00:01:25.545 --> 00:01:28.335
The fact that they're all different
colors, one beside the other,

00:01:28.335 --> 00:01:30.135
doesn't help me learn them.

00:01:30.765 --> 00:01:34.815
The same is true if we talk about,
you know, the parts of the body

00:01:35.265 --> 00:01:42.285
or numbers or the months of the
year, or the names of relatives.

00:01:42.285 --> 00:01:47.295
You know, the cousin, boy cousin,
girl cousin, uh, in-law, mother's

00:01:47.295 --> 00:01:51.030
side, father's side, you know,
wife's side, husband's side, all

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:55.650
of these things that are kind of
similar, their similarity makes them

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.900
more difficult to learn, thing...

00:01:57.905 --> 00:01:58.350
to me.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.500
And I'd be interested in fact in,
in hearing from people, whether

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.760
they have the same experience.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.020
If you learn things that are all
different from each other, it's easier.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:13.230
So, and if we can space the colors or
connect the colors to something else

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:19.995
like a plant or a red shirt, and we come
across them in different situations,

00:02:20.385 --> 00:02:23.055
then eventually we assimilate those.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.675
We start to learn them.

00:02:24.885 --> 00:02:29.205
But if we try to learn them as a list,
I find that very difficult to do.

00:02:29.505 --> 00:02:35.805
So any situation where things are
presented to us as a batch of things

00:02:35.805 --> 00:02:38.025
to learn, it makes it more difficult.

00:02:38.265 --> 00:02:41.085
Now, and the same goes
with synonyms, for example.

00:02:41.445 --> 00:02:46.125
Or, uh, you'll sometimes get a
list of false friends, for example,

00:02:46.125 --> 00:02:51.525
and supposed to, we're supposed to
notice how these, uh, false friends

00:02:51.525 --> 00:02:55.785
don't mean in the new language what
they meant in the old language, but

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.335
going over them as a, as a list.

00:02:58.605 --> 00:03:01.455
Here's a list of words that mean
something in language A, here's

00:03:01.455 --> 00:03:06.225
what they mean in language B, that
connection from A to B doesn't help

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.505
me learn them has been my experience.

00:03:08.895 --> 00:03:14.475
Uh, even things like, you know,
phrasal verbs that have the same verb.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:22.230
Get, uh, get out of, get into, get on, get
up, get rid of the fact that they all have

00:03:22.455 --> 00:03:25.185
the word get there may not be helpful.

00:03:25.185 --> 00:03:28.105
Now I'm not, I haven't been in the
position of having to learn phrasal

00:03:28.125 --> 00:03:33.285
verbs, but whenever I see these lists of
things that are similar, have the same

00:03:33.585 --> 00:03:36.105
component in them, it, it's interesting.

00:03:36.345 --> 00:03:41.475
It's interesting while I'm looking at
that list, and, and it may, in fact,

00:03:41.475 --> 00:03:43.454
reviewing them as a list may help me.

00:03:43.484 --> 00:03:44.325
I do that.

00:03:44.325 --> 00:03:49.545
It's an activity that I do, but I think
it's a minor part of the learning process.

00:03:49.575 --> 00:03:51.704
It's a review part of
the learning process.

00:03:51.855 --> 00:03:56.054
In order to learn those things, we
actually have to find them, you know,

00:03:56.054 --> 00:04:02.084
scattered, dispersed in content of
interest and, you know, see it here,

00:04:02.084 --> 00:04:03.674
forget it, see it there, forget it.

00:04:03.825 --> 00:04:10.679
And if we have enough input and if we
come across similar things, but we come

00:04:10.679 --> 00:04:15.839
across them in isolated situations, in
different contexts, we're more likely

00:04:15.839 --> 00:04:22.950
to learn them, whereas any attempt to
learn these similar things as a list is

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.409
probably not going to work that well.

00:04:25.469 --> 00:04:29.880
At least that's been my experience, and
I think it relates somewhat, somewhat to

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:35.615
this idea of interleaving versus block
learning, that anytime you're trying to

00:04:35.615 --> 00:04:40.115
master something by reading it over and
over again, whether it be a history paper

00:04:40.115 --> 00:04:45.995
or a list of words, that's less effective
than finding it over here, finding it

00:04:45.995 --> 00:04:48.305
over there, sort of grazing, interleaving.

00:04:48.605 --> 00:04:53.135
So I leave that as a thought and I also
will believe you with a video that I've

00:04:53.135 --> 00:04:59.685
done on interleaving and also one on,
you know, why we shouldn't, uh, engage

00:04:59.685 --> 00:05:01.575
in sort of intensive, block learning.

00:05:01.905 --> 00:05:04.755
So, uh, thank you for listening and
I look forward to your comments.

00:05:04.965 --> 00:05:05.525
Bye for now.

